#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105577 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/id #uuid "2eb23241-bd2b-5b76-b885-25cd3a51c16d" :question/math-academy-id "e-392" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=(2x-4)^2+2$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $\\Delta x=0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/94/94618262fb303035cd03ccf0274694d7083d9cd3a23d4231e3a8fccde8ac4fe1.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $11$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(11+6+3+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =22\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/id #uuid "41e80d7f-8a72-5bbf-9f66-c0047b28e45b" :question/math-academy-id "e-394" :question/problem "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[1,4].$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8.5$ | $10$ | $8$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[1,4].$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is computed as \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}\t\\approx\tf(2)\\Delta x_1+f(3)\\Delta x_2+f(3.5)\\Delta x_3+f(4)\\Delta x_4,\n$$\n\nwhere each $\\Delta x_i$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\Delta x_i = x_{i}-x_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a table of values that include the $\\Delta x$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Delta x$ | $-$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $8.5$ | $10$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈4\\cdot 1+8.5\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 0.5+8\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4+8.5+5+4 \\\\  & =21.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105648 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/id #uuid "c1deb898-8696-537f-8fb2-ae0f354dec29" :question/math-academy-id "e-7231" :question/problem "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-4,2].$ The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $12.$ What must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $k$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula for the right Riemann sum:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(-2)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $k$ | $2$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+k+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $\\mathcal A$ must be $12,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n2k+10 & =12\\[5pt]\n      2k &= 12-10\\[5pt]\n        k &= \\dfrac{2}{2}\\[5pt]\n         k  &= 1.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105649 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Right Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/id #uuid "1f74cbf1-c3f5-5ac2-ad55-ad22d7dae3ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-7233" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=(2x-4)^2+2$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $\\Delta x=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/94/94618262fb303035cd03ccf0274694d7083d9cd3a23d4231e3a8fccde8ac4fe1.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $11$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(11+6+3+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =22\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[0,2],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "878774eb-c7dd-5a87-917e-76cf95c77c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535651} {:answer/id #uuid "afa5e8c5-9f06-528f-8ca9-ee3e619e89fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535704} {:answer/id #uuid "d1153c2a-51a4-5211-961e-435b0d35c3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535752} {:answer/id #uuid "d228caf4-6c0e-5539-84ab-bf4720527798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535757} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ba9520-4205-5f57-8874-c6ed0a363e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "878774eb-c7dd-5a87-917e-76cf95c77c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535651} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2488740-ed19-518a-aec2-7d83e9f3a9a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fbb26d4-3faf-5389-98e4-54735a0cfc0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115697" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+2x$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/0b/0b9dd2388a7b60866eb15d336d51d181853fe5ac0e52dcd3e02f53fdd4848532.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $0.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(0.75+1+0.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Right Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16319f99-8be9-5064-9c7f-efbb6a452fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/55/5515a9fc4b43abb4cead1ebb3d6510e1476659b60ea62bc5088ffb6033f03efd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535470} {:answer/id #uuid "2282f659-8ccb-5cd3-9e78-6fa526890b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/54da2c81b258a8b4768ff8550e261c1f8997c64df40e83160e674d9d223c601e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535489} {:answer/id #uuid "29f7df40-eeb1-51c2-8680-69cf3b624d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8cca0bc83b39fe234e7c03bd55514fd6509e29810ad3641fd7d883d0758543c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535500} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4c7bdc-943f-55e4-89c6-49531cc23512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f3/f302cc1e806f082574c0f4929d2963d90f349c267d7421ad3853842f789fa8a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535684} {:answer/id #uuid "d08f46eb-c4d7-5a3d-86b5-5fbdff207392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e7c9f3684c8afaf638e130dee31be8375ffafb271cc2b0e8a0e74c87578ac3aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2282f659-8ccb-5cd3-9e78-6fa526890b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/54da2c81b258a8b4768ff8550e261c1f8997c64df40e83160e674d9d223c601e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535489} :answer-field/id #uuid "b61e2068-20db-58c0-8c08-999445737816" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f185512a-6bdc-56ba-b826-59b4b54f5782" :question/math-academy-id "q-115700" :question/problem "A right Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-2$ and $x=0$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *underestimate* of the area?" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum underestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is decreasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only function that is decreasing over $x\\in [-2,0]$ is as follows.\n\n![](images/54/54da2c81b258a8b4768ff8550e261c1f8997c64df40e83160e674d9d223c601e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01050c59-9553-589f-9c48-f5d7d72b8958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,220$ flour bags" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535437} {:answer/id #uuid "40d7d78b-066c-592b-9585-d48edeb29892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,920$ flour bags" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535533} {:answer/id #uuid "80f4d759-7dd2-58ba-b661-c508f8284e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,400$ flour bags" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535639} {:answer/id #uuid "d17f65ac-d738-5edc-866a-4144aae04c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,100$ flour bags" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535755} {:answer/id #uuid "f66356a1-a60a-5a2c-9291-629272352032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,600$ flour bags" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80f4d759-7dd2-58ba-b661-c508f8284e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,400$ flour bags" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535639} :answer-field/id #uuid "60b2584c-7157-5847-8956-d79d5f653369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7984804e-ee09-5ab8-9ea3-cfb9bf84394d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115745" :question/problem "The following table shows the rate $r(t)$, in flour bags per minute, at which a machine makes flour over a period of $75$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $15$ | $30$ | $45$ | $60$ | $75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r\\,\\text{(flour bags/minute)}$ | $30$ | $25$ | $35$ | $40$ | $35$ | $25$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum for $r(t)$ over the interval $[0,75]$, with five subintervals, to approximate the number of flour bags made by the machine in $75$ minutes." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=75,$ and there are $n=5$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{75-0}{5}=15.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{r(15)+r(30)+r(45)+r(60)+r(75)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $15$ | $30$ | $45$ | $60$ | $75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $25$ | $35$ | $40$ | $35$ | $25$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+35+40+35+25)\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =160\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =2\\,400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of flour bags made by the machine in $75$ minutes.  So, the number of flour bags is approximately $2\\,400.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52322dcb-1905-5fca-972d-928776fd905b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,254$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535564} {:answer/id #uuid "5feeab5a-e0af-5745-817b-3debbe2734b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,314$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535585} {:answer/id #uuid "64f4abe0-1e42-5107-8805-9c199b4747bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,442$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535593} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e1c334-289e-56bd-8da7-42c0271dacf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,384$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535807} {:answer/id #uuid "fd87f624-d66a-5360-92e5-fbca297f8972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,184$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2e1c334-289e-56bd-8da7-42c0271dacf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,384$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535807} :answer-field/id #uuid "4897e11e-46e1-5d7f-9ea0-0508e0dce0ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6772ccf8-308a-5e57-b20e-bbe6b51388bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-20315" :question/problem "A drone has positive velocity $v(t)$ after being flown upward from an initial height of $0$ feet at time  $t=0$ seconds. The velocity of the drone is recorded for selected values of $t$ over the interval $0\\le t\\le 40$ seconds, as shown in the table below. \n\n| $t\\,\\text{(seconds)}$ | $0$ | $7$ | $15$ | $25$ | $32$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v\\,\\text{(feet/second)}$ | $3$ | $10$ | $25$ | $40$ | $46$ | $49$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[0,40]$ with five subintervals to approximate the total distance covered by the drone.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the velocity-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=v(t)$ over $[0,40].$ \nIn this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the total distance can be approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈v(7)Δ{t}_{1}+v(15)Δ{t}_{2}+v(25)Δ{t}_{3}+v(32)Δ{t}_{4}+v(40)Δ{t}_{5},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $7$ | $15$ | $25$ | $32$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $7$ | $8$ | $10$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| $v(t)$ | $-$ | $10$ | $25$ | $40$ | $46$ | $49$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the number of liters and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈10\\cdot 7+25\\cdot 8+40\\cdot 10+46\\cdot 7+49\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =70+200+400+322+392 \\\\  & =1\\,384.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance covered by the drone. So, the distance is approximately $1\\,384$ feet."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Right Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17e7ee46-c0a0-5388-b5c3-f743c9eeeff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.25,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535472} {:answer/id #uuid "8491a6b6-27ba-5890-9894-2dbd35131e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.25,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535644} {:answer/id #uuid "bae36594-f50c-5335-98b1-7ed2a0ea25e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.50,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535721} {:answer/id #uuid "e21421ad-d404-568a-b6ff-34207debc096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.25,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535783} {:answer/id #uuid "ee374620-40ba-583d-8548-625378a12bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.25,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee374620-40ba-583d-8548-625378a12bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.25,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535802} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef28899d-b306-5e8e-a4d3-bef109a6e779" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10bc8e6f-4c2c-5a63-aa64-53c9dbd12a9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-20794" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/5c/5c52bbe248df0e5a595298d978276023bcb681daa904f37e2818013687aab52e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.75$ | $3$ | $1.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.75+3+1.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =8.50\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[0,2],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b60d1c76-997e-5fea-b00b-ccb5d53f5fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "620" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b60d1c76-997e-5fea-b00b-ccb5d53f5fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "620" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535714} :answer-field/id #uuid "14d74c82-2922-5d9b-8164-dac9534530d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18df9f2e-656d-5486-9ae1-27119d2f448d" :question/math-academy-id "q-316517" :question/problem "The following table shows the water flow rate, $R(t),$ through a pipe (in liters per minute) over a period of $40$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R\\,\\text{(liters/minute)}$ | $12$ | $15$ | $17$ | $16$ | $14$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum for $R(t)$ over the interval $[0,40]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total volume of water pumped.\n\nThe total volume is \n{{field-1}} liters." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=40,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{40-0}{4}=10.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R(10)+R(20)+R(30)+R(40)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $R(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $15$ | $17$ | $16$ | $14$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(15+17+16+14)\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =62\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =620\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total volume of water pumped over a period of $40$ minutes. So, the total volume is $620$ liters."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7196d172-1722-59c9-8d40-bba7f5842eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7196d172-1722-59c9-8d40-bba7f5842eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535607} :answer-field/id #uuid "53ef4dc0-4c26-5272-80ba-0abca0bed270" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e5afc97-cdbf-5609-994c-66b16cc595b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-346101" :question/problem "The following table shows the fuel consumption rate, $F(t),$ of a generator (in liters per hour) over a period of $8$ hours.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(hours)}$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F\\,\\text{(liters/hour)}$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum for $F(t)$ over the interval $[0,8]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total amount of fuel used.\n\nThe total amount of fuel is \n{{field-1}} liters." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=8,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{8-0}{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{F(2)+F(4)+F(6)+F(8)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $F(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(t)$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+5+2+6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total amount of fuel used over a period of $8$ hours. So, the total amount of fuel is $32$ liters."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a4bda3f-13a2-588d-8d36-9540552bfd8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a4bda3f-13a2-588d-8d36-9540552bfd8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535523} :answer-field/id #uuid "438cb7c6-cefd-5543-b39d-e0a02ae170e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8de05636-df00-52b0-a8f6-5c5d3db73b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-346443" :question/problem "The following table shows the water drainage rate of a reservoir over a period of $8$ hours, starting at $2\\,\\text{p.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{gallons/hour})$ | $2$ | $k$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nIf the right Riemann sum approximation of the total water drained from $2\\,\\text{p.m}$ to $10\\,\\text{p.m.}$ has a value of $30$ gallons, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve (rate vs. time) can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R(4)+R(6)+R(8)+R(10)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of the rate at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $k$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+5+4+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+12) \\\\  & =2k+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total water drained from $2\\,\\text{p.m}$ to $10\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total drainage is $30$ gallons, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+24 & =30 \\\\ 2k & =30-24 \\\\ k & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dcef793-e9bd-548e-8171-f1c2694d465b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dcef793-e9bd-548e-8171-f1c2694d465b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535461} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3ccd5f4-a37a-56a2-9400-43d3c5f9830f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "295aa24d-cc18-5ddf-b640-8d50f96f1e1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-346446" :question/problem "The following table shows the cooling rate of a walk-in freezer over a period of $10$ hours, starting at $9\\,\\text{a.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ | $15$ | $17$ | $19$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{kilojoules/hour})$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $k$ |\n\n If the right Riemann sum approximation of the total energy removed from $9\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $7\\,\\text{p.m.}$ has a value of $40$ kilojoules, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve (rate vs. time) can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R(11)+R(13)+R(15)+R(17)+R(19)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of the rate at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $11$ | $13$ | $15$ | $17$ | $19$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(4+3+2+4+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+13) \\\\  & =2k+26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total energy removed from $9\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $7\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total energy removed is $40$ kilojoules, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+26 & =40 \\\\ 2k & =40-26 \\\\ k & =\\frac{14}{2} \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce97e8ae-fc3e-5356-86d9-e5bc39af2fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce97e8ae-fc3e-5356-86d9-e5bc39af2fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535746} :answer-field/id #uuid "af709f83-13b1-5d97-bf30-3739018c740d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b623f34-55bd-5cf9-90b7-bc3a51b954e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-346462" :question/problem "The table below gives the medication infusion rate $r(t)$, in milligrams per minute, for a patient.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $18$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)\\,\\text{(mg/min)}$ | $6$ | $5$ | $3$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[0,18]$ with five subintervals to approximate the total medication delivered (in milligrams).\n\nThe total medication delivered is\n{{field-1}} milligrams." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=r(t)$ over $[0,18].$ In this case, we have $n=5$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is computed as \n\n$$\nA≈r(4)Δ{t}_{1}+r(7)Δ{t}_{2}+r(10)Δ{t}_{3}+r(13)Δ{t}_{4}+r(18)Δ{t}_{5},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $18$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $4$ | $3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| $r(t)$ | $-$ | $5$ | $3$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈5\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =20+9+12+6+5 \\\\  & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total accumulation of medication. So, the total medication delivered is $52$ milligrams."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07357d7a-2f83-5eb7-92b8-b510a24224cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535449} {:answer/id #uuid "1894293b-2266-5249-81b6-4ceec74dc5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535473} {:answer/id #uuid "34734b2d-4a62-530e-a6bd-694b62c062aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535513} {:answer/id #uuid "41113075-3263-5183-b6a6-417bd1862926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535534} {:answer/id #uuid "b72a414b-c009-5184-b3b0-8ca0bca96051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07357d7a-2f83-5eb7-92b8-b510a24224cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535449} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3c699ed-502d-50f7-85eb-b0f04825f3f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d36beb24-ba8a-5d0f-80e1-d03ab3463ddd" :question/math-academy-id "q-49092" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[0,15]$ with a regular step size. If the right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $60$, then what must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $k$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(5)+f(10)+f(15)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+4+k)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(7+k)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5k+35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be  $60,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+35 & =60 \\\\ 5k & =60-35 \\\\ k & =\\frac{25}{5} \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Right Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "483b31fe-308f-54cf-b9da-7c12fd2b107c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af940537c48dd51fd8b30ea6123bf0a5feaba3b0e18cb1689e39cb4c6e6e3f08.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535542} {:answer/id #uuid "a7aa5f8f-adeb-576e-88ec-7221029a3ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/28/2878610f5f3ffba093918f49277648d9a72860cc7d2ca6f3f6d51b57149a04a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535695} {:answer/id #uuid "aca90562-2290-57cf-b1b1-d39efed6d256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b28645afdc7194c6cc90c74cb2cd424379aba3ea1a63d42c1431c6924aa08c3d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535703} {:answer/id #uuid "e86fcf6c-4ab9-51fd-a963-8f76ec79c9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/1287968881b822c475474e0a1e89ed589473d24f7cdf278c6d9fbd18b960344b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535792} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e422a3-30ab-534f-8904-0b31f55ffbf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/98/98536b689340c85721e38b82c34abdbe060c851014493127fa2c67c600228dfc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e86fcf6c-4ab9-51fd-a963-8f76ec79c9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/1287968881b822c475474e0a1e89ed589473d24f7cdf278c6d9fbd18b960344b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535792} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6eace52-de2d-55a8-a63f-a445d7ee7371" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7189171-aff4-5525-9ffc-edd7b878fae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-49115" :question/problem "A right Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-2$ and $x=1$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *underestimate* of the area?" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum underestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is decreasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only function that is decreasing over $x\\in [-2,1]$ is as follows.\n\n![](images/12/1287968881b822c475474e0a1e89ed589473d24f7cdf278c6d9fbd18b960344b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Right Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bd814a6-30d6-59ac-a780-e4809fcd45e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a672268875752204a3f8332a1761cc0482687b5469c5b9ddd45044e8e0fa2b39.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104363} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b95a77-626a-5888-945f-ead2bb6eb3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4d4e96c9dc96dcfc8ef6c2fe984a915ff047531ce33706dd781a9d159ca82950.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104364} {:answer/id #uuid "2925a444-5ec3-524b-bca1-b952f071d65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/19/1929cc971dec72740c30825bae06c5119b8055da6d4f2bd37e9654bb84542b39.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104365} {:answer/id #uuid "3529a846-a5aa-50fd-8996-d967d0623533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1a3070d66463de310537f519a992a6357908597e2661249a72a11cf6582d019c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104366} {:answer/id #uuid "54d038e6-6f3a-5bf7-a195-1d9bbd720b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d525a78713b4f34c4fda8e2ac8e74a665691fd114a033f7727b91435bf5124fb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7b95a77-626a-5888-945f-ead2bb6eb3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4d4e96c9dc96dcfc8ef6c2fe984a915ff047531ce33706dd781a9d159ca82950.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104364} :answer-field/id #uuid "87dde1bd-f911-5689-afee-6d9b1969b89c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa1b07db-8546-5dcc-a57c-3d4bbe55271b" :question/math-academy-id "q-49117" :question/problem "A right Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-3$ and $x=-1$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *overestimate* of the area?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum overestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is increasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only function that is increasing over $x\\in [-3,-1]$ is as follows.\n\n![](images/4d/4d4e96c9dc96dcfc8ef6c2fe984a915ff047531ce33706dd781a9d159ca82950.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e0e2534-557c-5d1d-a4c1-a63a3979c0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105059} {:answer/id #uuid "a5dd965c-513e-5c45-be86-386b4fde7bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105060} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f8f7a3-d0f4-5000-b70c-55ece98dc00f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105061} {:answer/id #uuid "e569548f-e5e4-5a19-a049-83ace359cba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105062} {:answer/id #uuid "59352711-4b84-54f5-8f21-16affdcef33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e0e2534-557c-5d1d-a4c1-a63a3979c0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105059} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9bbd5d1-52fc-5532-9c11-a0ca2391cb50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d9f3e81-c696-5e30-a1a6-32de40a197d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74324" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=4-2x$ over the interval $[0,1]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.25.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/c4/c478dd4988aaf23348c2453e1f9fca137ddd33284cde7cb7eae1775982bd4b2e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.25)+f(0.5)+f(0.75)+f(1)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.5$ | $3$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.5+3+2.5+2)\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =11\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.75\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Right Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002a6a31-b58c-5580-9148-3001495a4d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535435} {:answer/id #uuid "42e96206-5932-5b4b-b71e-f61b37f74d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535539} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebf40b5-4be5-5ebe-9a26-6c0c7cbdff35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535604} {:answer/id #uuid "7d68cf48-ca72-5f17-9243-c2b8260ae54c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535629} {:answer/id #uuid "e5aa91ea-a144-5bc9-bee2-f4c14cf6d192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "002a6a31-b58c-5580-9148-3001495a4d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535435} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ee3ca7-e743-5c98-a18c-ba847bc17d29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e633987-af32-5b5c-bd64-b66c42ee580f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74337" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=2x+3$ over the interval $[-1,1]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/be/be5f6207531c18acecdb6a7ba3890e23913de4a78546db620f2986dd0467fa6d.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-0.5)+f(0)+f(0.5)+f(1)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(2+3+4+5)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =14\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[-1,1],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1649648b-d221-5e0a-8f9d-630907a81603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339324} {:answer/id #uuid "173114fb-816a-522c-8b10-d7a5c6b86cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339334} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb672c4-690f-5f25-a8a4-44bb1e8ddbd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340087} {:answer/id #uuid "a83683d8-5ae3-5945-b911-de4f9feb8c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340654} {:answer/id #uuid "d77d48dc-cd96-56f7-a04a-d30082752caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a83683d8-5ae3-5945-b911-de4f9feb8c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340654} :answer-field/id #uuid "1347de4e-e87f-5553-b355-971c6eb6c1a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc8544c2-610f-58ff-bd57-2903f678a6bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-74348" :question/problem "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(5.5)Δ{x}_{1}+f(8)Δ{x}_{2}+f(9)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $1$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈4\\cdot 1.5+6\\cdot 2.5+9.5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6+15+9.5 \\\\  & =30.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d833ab3-a2d7-56c6-b9a4-76279b982fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535502} {:answer/id #uuid "3955f388-dc66-59c7-b098-71ac23637885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535522} {:answer/id #uuid "b73287af-ab26-51ab-b425-1e0dc0ef802c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535717} {:answer/id #uuid "e089c53a-d6d4-5d12-a806-355ad6596021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535781} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4f37bd-b672-575b-9376-0431108bd573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d833ab3-a2d7-56c6-b9a4-76279b982fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535502} :answer-field/id #uuid "c465fa60-6711-58fc-9089-6170878ac599" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae6e559e-361b-5b37-89a9-1d035fa07b1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74350" :question/problem "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[2,6].$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $8$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[2,6].$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(3.5)Δ{x}_{1}+f(4)Δ{x}_{2}+f(6)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $1.5$ | $0.5$ | $2$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈2\\cdot 1.5+3.5\\cdot 0.5+8\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3+1.75+16 \\\\  & =20.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f49d01a-1356-56f8-a283-b3d4f4a0d947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535529} {:answer/id #uuid "5f512ef4-db13-54a0-9ad5-4aa3f26a6f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535584} {:answer/id #uuid "79d0dca6-5d5f-58d5-a25e-55114ac39c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535624} {:answer/id #uuid "7e286349-c3c1-55a1-8853-0b796282bc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535632} {:answer/id #uuid "93b84c32-fa45-5736-abc6-07c50d96d933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f49d01a-1356-56f8-a283-b3d4f4a0d947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535529} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c8ae8ea-1630-59da-a400-3740079028cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16561015-1e48-5ef8-9194-b46c8f058fda" :question/math-academy-id "q-74351" :question/problem "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[1,7].$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2.5$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $5$ | $8.5$ | $9$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[1,7].$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(2.5)Δ{x}_{1}+f(5)Δ{x}_{2}+f(7)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2.5$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $5$ | $8.5$ | $9$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈5\\cdot 1.5+8.5\\cdot 2.5+9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7.5+21.25+18 \\\\  & =46.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8c4dd0-b4fb-5f1d-970c-93e234d6c211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535460} {:answer/id #uuid "11aa1a77-5ad4-5bbc-8d24-035cfb0fffc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535465} {:answer/id #uuid "515a0644-5d83-50b2-b738-12e33dae1bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535560} {:answer/id #uuid "a0746224-bc7e-5b08-b957-2482f2935120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535685} {:answer/id #uuid "d73cdc93-d689-5be0-b050-c70c2fe6ce12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d8c4dd0-b4fb-5f1d-970c-93e234d6c211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535460} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f5bea90-ec71-5ac4-a16e-8e74f85dff8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86b94149-e5f8-5f16-b1b9-921b18ee69eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-86344" :question/problem "The following table gives the speed of a minivan measured during a $4$-second interval.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{seconds})$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{meters/second})$ | $17$ | $18$ | $k$ | $25$ | $27$ |\n\n If the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance that the minivan traveled during the $4$-second interval has a value of $85$ meters, what is the value of $k?$\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{v(1)+v(2)+v(3)+v(4)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $18$ | $k$ | $25$ | $27$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(18+k+25+27)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =70+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance that the minivan traveled. Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance is $85$ meters,  we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70+k & =85 \\\\ k & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "240701c7-693d-5227-90b8-2a6284ad88fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535491} {:answer/id #uuid "57bfefc1-dc04-51d9-be61-49334d95d8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535575} {:answer/id #uuid "8cec8f0e-46be-5259-81e8-bcdb5a4a53a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535659} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fcc852-79de-51f7-9ec6-886cb9fb714d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535767} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2ae6d8-8214-5546-b570-caa5929f2b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57bfefc1-dc04-51d9-be61-49334d95d8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535575} :answer-field/id #uuid "7023ddcf-d3c1-52ff-bd11-0bc5ea2c28b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbb4c94f-2803-5b7e-b57a-776b92b59056" :question/math-academy-id "q-86372" :question/problem "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(t)$ on the closed interval $[10,40].$ The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(t)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $480.$ What must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $t$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(t)$ | $18$ | $12$ | $k$ | $16$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(20)+f(30)+f(40)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(t)$ | $12$ | $k$ | $16$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(12+k+16)\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =(28+k)\\cdot 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $480$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(28+k)\\cdot 10 & =480 \\\\ 28+k & =48 \\\\ k & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
